# Accounts are durable but NOT publishable.
#
# They survive a substrate wipe for the same reason worlds and personalities do —
# they are read once at start and written back on change — but they must never
# reach the remote. This repository is public, and an account record carries a
# real email address and the identity provider's stable subject id. Git history
# is the one place you cannot quietly remove something from later, so the
# decision has to be made before the first write, not after the first user.
#
# Worlds and personalities have no such problem: they are fiction, and being
# diffable and reviewable is the whole point of putting them here. They live in
# a mind now (`--mind`), so what lands here is whatever a daemon run without one
# is given.
accounts/

# `npcd` holds no key — sign-in belongs to the gateway, which never shares the
# one it signs sessions with. Kept as a rule so that anything later dropped here
# stays out of a public repository by default rather than by remembering.
secrets/

# Which co-resident models this machine has, and where. Absolute paths into
# somebody's own disk — the same reason `models.override.yaml` is ignored at the
# workspace root. The *shape* of the file is documented in `src/guests.rs`,
# which is where a reader should learn it from; a checked-in copy would only be
# one machine's answer, and the wrong one for every other machine.
guests.yaml

# Portraits, as uploaded. Content-addressed, so the name is the hash and a
# re-upload of the same picture is the same file — but they are still whatever a
# user chose to send, at whatever size they chose to send it, which is neither
# reviewable nor something a public repository should carry. A character's
# record names the id; the bytes stay on the machine that received them.
images/

# …except the keyer's fixtures, which are the opposite case in every respect.
#
# They are this daemon's *own* output, drawn from prompts and seeds written down
# beside them in `cutout::fixtures`, so they are reproducible rather than
# received, reviewable rather than arbitrary, and fiction rather than anybody's
# data. They have to be tracked because they are the test: every threshold in
# `src/cutout.rs` was moved by something a synthetic square of colour could not
# show — a wall lit unevenly, a jumper the colour of the wall's shadow, green
# bounced into hair — and a test that cannot be run by the next person is not a
# test. 1.6 MB, once.
!tests/images/

# The narrative clock and anything else that moves on its own. Committing a
# value that advances 60× per second would leave the tree permanently dirty.
state/

# The conversation substrate's redo log — the segmented append-only files
# `/v1/substrate/storage` reports on. An engine running here writes it into the
# data directory, which defaults to this one. Segments reach hundreds of MB and
# rewrite continuously, so they are the worst possible thing to have tracked.
.substrate/
